#---
# purpose: "A dispatch test reads a cons cell's first element, so lists of different element types are different questions"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.107 step 3. A list test used to see empty-or-cons and nothing else,
# so `[integer]` and `[:ok | :err]` were one and the same question and the
# clause written first took every value: this printed `1 1 2 2`, and which
# answer you got was which clause you wrote first. That is the same defect
# `dispatch_annotated_tuple_tag_clauses` pins for tuple tags, one axis over.
#
# The test now asks a cons cell's HEAD its own question, so the two clauses
# are two questions and both orders print the same two lines.
#
# ## The head is a ONE-SIDED FILTER, and the last four lines are why
#
# A list type is homogeneous -- one element type for the whole list -- so a
# head OUTSIDE the question proves the value outside the surface, while a head
# INSIDE it proves nothing about the tail no test reads. Rejection is exact;
# acceptance is not.
#
# The four `Enum.all?` lines settle FOUR arms at one
# `List.reduce_while_cont/3` callsite, one per element type:
#
#     A   [:false | :true]        Enum.all?([true, false])
#     B   [int | :ok | :true]     Enum.all?([true, 1, :ok])
#     C   [int]                   Enum.all?([1, 2])
#     D   [int | :ok]             Enum.all?([1, :ok])
#
# The callsite TESTS TWO of them, A and B. C and D are dropped, because B
# stands in for each -- the same callee, a strictly wider surface, a test that
# admits everything theirs admits -- and no seat would ever place either ahead
# of B: their heads overlap B's at an int, so the pair is erasing there, and
# only B's surface names what theirs hold in the tail no test reads. An arm the
# seat would never place ahead of its stand-in is no destination at all
# (fz-kdt.143), so C and D go and `[1, :ok]` -- the fourth line -- reaches B,
# which is the only body an int-headed list can reach here and the one that
# named those values.
#
# Dropping the ARM is not dropping the code. C's and D's specializations are
# still in the artifact and still natively defined, reached by direct call
# edges the callable-flow path grounds at the `Enum.all?([1, 2])` and
# `Enum.all?([1, :ok])` lines; `fz2 --emit=stats run` reads 111
# `fz.codegen.define_function.stop` either way. Only the callsite's choice
# between them goes.
#
# A and B are what is left, and they are the residue. Their heads overlap at
# `:true` and neither surface contains the other, so no seat is escape-free,
# neither stands in for the other, and arrival order decides which is tested
# first. That is fz-kdt.131's facet 3, this fixture is its written-down
# reproducer, and 131 owns it; `enum_predicate_search` carries the corpus's
# other two.
#
# STATICALLY REAL, DYNAMICALLY UNREACHED. The pair is a seat the compiler
# cannot make safe, and no value in this program crosses it. `Enum.all?` folds
# through `List.reduce_while_cont([head | tail], acc, reducer)`, which hands the
# TAIL to the recursive dispatch, so the whole `[true, 1, :ok]` never arrives at
# a dispatch at all -- what reaches the A arm is `[false]` and `[]`, both inside
# `[:false | :true]`. Measured at fz-kdt.144 with the surface-membership
# tripwire, which walks every element of an admitted list against the clause its
# head answered: this fixture reads 0 escapes at the settled arrival and under
# `arms:reverse`, `arms:1` .. `arms:6` and every `wrappers:` setting. The one
# arrival in the corpus that reaches a facet-3 pair with a real value is
# `enum_predicate_search` under `arms:6`, where `[1, :ok]` runs a `[integer]`
# body.
#
# History, because the defect is what the fixture is for. Measured at
# f69c5ea26, before the head question existed, all four arms were one question
# and fz-kdt.118's drop removed C and D inside that one group -- so neither was
# a destination and no arrival could seat one first. What DID abort was the
# settled order and arm seeds 1/2/3/4/6: an int element read by the
# [:false | :true] body (`fz_unbox_atom`, exit 134 on `run` and `build`).
# `interp` survived on its dynamic tags, which is why this is a
# three-path-parity fixture and not a stdout one.
fn kind(xs :: [integer]), do: 1
fn kind(xs :: [:ok | :err]), do: 2

fn dnik(xs :: [:ok | :err]), do: 2
fn dnik(xs :: [integer]), do: 1

fn pick(n) do
  if n > 0 do
    [n, n]
  else
    [:ok, :err]
  end
end

fn main() do
  dbg(kind(pick(1)))
  dbg(kind(pick(0)))
  dbg(dnik(pick(1)))
  dbg(dnik(pick(0)))

  dbg(Enum.all?([true, false]))
  dbg(Enum.all?([true, 1, :ok]))
  dbg(Enum.all?([1, 2]))
  dbg(Enum.all?([1, :ok]))
end
